2202818393707, "top": [[" Windows", -0.3742202818393707], [" older", -2.6650137901306152]]}, {"t": " Intel", "lp": -0.16543853282928467, "top": [[" Intel", -0.16543853282928467], [" drivers", -2.6846933364868164]]}, {"t": " Arc", "lp": -0.07991808652877808, "top": [[" Arc", -0.07991808652877808], [" Graphics", -2.9539263248443604]]}, {"t": " drivers", "lp": -0.033138457685709, "top": [[" drivers", -0.033138457685709], [" driver", -3.607229471206665]]}, {"t": ").", "lp": -0.1313875913619995, "top": [[").", -0.1313875913619995], [" have", -2.132852554321289]]}, {"t": "\n\n", "lp": -1.0543076992034912, "top": [["\n\n", -1.0543076992034912], [" The", -1.0738236904144287]]}, {"t": "The", "lp": -2.13192081451416, "top": [["##", -1.158046007156372], ["The", -2.13192081451416]]}, {"t": " most", "lp": -1.3007044792175293, "top": [[" most", -1.3007044792175293], [" mode", -2.2346434593200684]]}, {"t": " reliable", "lp": -0.051633674651384354, "top": [[" reliable", -0.051633674651384354], [" authoritative", -3.5595147609710693]]}, {"t": " way", "lp": -0.8671825528144836, "top": [[" way", -0.8671825528144836], [" guidance", -1.697087049484253]]}], "text": "# Xe GPU Compute Modes on Intel Arc\n\nThe `compute_mode` (or `ccs_mode`) parameter controls how the Xe driver handles **CCS** (Compute Control Subsystem) cores on your GPU. Let me explain what these actually mean.\n\n## Background: What is a CCS?\n\nIntel Arc GPUs have a hybrid architecture. In addition to the main **EU** (Execution Unit) cores that do the heavy compute/rendering work, there's a small set of dedicated **CCS cores**. These are lightweight cores that:\n- Handle command processing and scheduling\n- Manage low-priority work\n- Offload \"housekeeping\" tasks so the main EUs stay busy\n\nThe `compute_mode` parameter tells the driver how to distribute work across these cores.\n\n## The Modes\n\n**Mode 1 (Default):**\n- CCS cores are used for **low-priority queues** and command processing\n- Main EUs handle the primary compute/rendering load\n- This is the standard, balanced configuration\n\n**Mode 2:**\n- CCS cores are used more aggressively for compute work\n- Can help with certain compute-heavy workloads by offloading more to the CCS cores\n- May benefit specific GPU compute scenarios (like certain AI/ML inference or GPGPU tasks)\n\n## Mode 4\n\nThis is typically an **advanced or experimental** mode, often used for:\n- **Profiling and debugging**\n- **Isolating CCS cores** for dedicated work\n- Testing or development scenarios where you need fine-grained control over which cores handle which tasks\n\n## Important Caveat\n\nI want to be honest here: **the exact behavioral differences between these modes aren't well-documented in public Intel documentation**, and the naming/behavior has shifted across different driver versions (both the Linux `xe` driver and the Windows Intel Arc drivers).\n\nThe most reliable way"}